Prediction of pressure loss and heat transfer in cooling channels has been commonly and successfully addressed by onedimensional models, at least as long as a liquid is flowing in reasonably smooth channels. In LRE, however, when using hydrogen as the coolant, it is typically in the gaseous phase and phenomena like change of properties and thermal stratification must be taken into account [3,4]. Hydrogen has been commonly used, and it is a good coolant, therefore in hydrogen cooled engine design the goal has been mainly devoted to minimize pressure loss through suitably smoothed channels. Channel wall roughness cannot be completely removed, so the effect of a tiny roughness has to be included in modeling. It is worth noticing that even in the case of tiny roughness, a special model taking into account the different role of roughness on friction and heat transfer has been considered [4–6]. Such a model intends to separate more and more the heat transfer from the friction behavior as roughness increases. It has been shown that, despite the tiny value, roughness modeling can play a significant role in the wall temperature predictions [7]. Roughness, indeed, leads to an increase in friction and heat transfer, although heat transfer enhancement is less than the friction one because of the different physical phenomena [8,9]. In fact, while the friction
