5. Discussion
In this section the numerical results obtained using the present thermal correction for high roughness channels (THRC) with Spalart-Allmaras turbulence model are discussed. The effect of roughness is analyzed in terms of dimensionless velocity and temperature profiles, then the friction factor and Nusselt number values are shown compared to the theoretical values of a smooth pipe. Results obtained with THRC correction are also presented for:
1) comparison with correlations and the basic Aupoix approach at Pr number inside and outside of the calibration range; and 2) a test case representative of a liquid rocket engine cooling channel where methane is considered as a real fluid with variable properties. Figs. 10, 11 show the solutions obtained for the dimensionless velocity and temperature profiles ( P r = 6.03), respectively. The behavior of the dimensionless velocity profile at different roughness levels (Fig. 10) shows that the effect of roughness is well mimicked using the Boeing extension of the Spalart-Allmaras turbulence model [40]. Indeed, roughness has a major effect right at the wall, where the viscous sub-layer (0 < y+ < 5) is physically destabilized by the presence of the roughness elements. In particular, in the fully rough regime, the viscous sub-layer almost disappears entirely, accounting for the fact that the influence of viscosity on the hydrodynamic behavior of the flow is negligible. Above it, the logarithmic region is shifted downwards by the quantity U +(hs+), but it retains its original slope [24]. The effect of THRC correction is clearly visible from the profiles of the dimensionless temperature T + obtained for P r=6.03 with the assumption of smooth and rough pipe, respectively (Fig. 11). When the turbulent THRC correction is not applied, and therefore the turbulent Prandtl number is kept constant and equal to 0.9, the temperature shift T + with respect to the smooth curve is overestimated and follows the velocity profile. On the other hand, when the THRC correction is applied, it yields to a decreased heat transfer toward the wall, and, accordingly, the increase in the wall temperature T w results in a smaller temperature shift T +. Fig. 12 shows the increase in friction factor f D caused by roughness obtained for a flow with Re = 8.207×104. The numerical results of the reference grid are compared with the theoretical ones obtained using Eq. (9). Both theoretical and numerical values are normalized with respect to the theoretical value for the corresponding smooth tube, (hs/D=0). Numerical results have been evaluated in the fully developed region where the friction factor is constant, being in the fully rough regime. The friction factor increases sharply with increasing equivalent sand grain roughness height and directly influences the pressure loss. As mentioned in Section 4, numerical results are in good agreement with the theoretical ones until extremely high values of the relative pipe roughness. Results obtained with the THRC correction are shown in Fig. 13 in terms of the Nusselt number ratio, also normalized with respect to the theoretical value for smooth pipes Nu/Nu0, as a function of the relative pipe roughness hs/D. The reference value Nu0 is computed by the Dittus-Boelter equation, while numerical results have been computed in the fully developed region. According to the experimental results of [45], the impact of roughness on heat transfer varies with Prandtl number, due to the corresponding variation of the thickness of the heat conduction sublayer. Additionally, the effect of roughness on heat transfer performance tends to flatten out for high values of the equivalent sand grain roughness height [11,46]. The friction factor ratio f D / f D,0 discussed in Fig. 12 is also reported here for comparison. The comparison highlights the different behavior of friction factor and Nusselt number increase with increasing roughness. This comparison is made more evident in Fig. 14 showing the ratio between heat transfer and friction factor increase caused by roughness normalized with respect to the smooth channel. Flattening of the heat transfer increase and continuous increase of friction factor lead to the decrease of their ratio at high values of the relative pipe ratio roughness. It is worth noticing again that the effect of roughness is reduced for lower values of the Prandtl number. The results obtained with THRC are then compared to the original thermal correction presented by Aupoix in [27] for one of the calibration test cases. In particular, the test case consid
