Results are interesting as they show the ability of THRC correction to handle properly the changes of properties. It is also interesting to discuss the discrepancy with respect to the Dipprey and Sabersky correlation that occurs in the first part of the channel. At a first glance, it can be surprising as the THRC correction has been calibrated on that correlation. Indeed, this discrepancy should be expected. The results are in good agreement in the region where the changes of the Prandtl number in streamwise and crosswise direction are smaller, while in the first part of the channel the Prandtl number changes are larger. In a region of large sensitivity of Prandtl number changes with temperature, or in correspondence of high heat fluxes leading to large changes of temperature in the crosswise direction, the hypothesis and data under which the Dipprey and Sabersky correlation is developed are no longer valid. In principle, the effect of roughness is mainly driven by the phenomena occurring in the vicinity of the wall, so local rather than bulk properties should be used. Accordingly, THRC correction takes into account for the local properties. In general, however, the THRC correction gives results in line with expectations, showing a good agreement in a regime of constant properties. Such a result allows to consider the present correction as a general value correction for the application in circular cross section cooling channels with high roughness. On the other hand, in case of changing properties, the availability of suitable experimental or DNS data would be of support to confirm that the predictions of THRC corrected rough model of Spalart-Allmaras RANS closure are more robust than their calibration source.
6. Conclusions
Following the approach proposed by Aupoix [27], a thermal correction based on the correlation of Dipprey and Sabersky has been calibrated and implemented to predict realistic values of the convective heat transfer coefficient in high roughness cooling channels. The correction is based on the turbulent Prandtl number increase in the wall region, which allows to decrease the wall heat transfer. The latter would be highly overestimated at high roughness with the basic extension of equivalent sand grain approach to account for wall roughness. At present, the limited amount of experimental or Direct Numerical Simulation data only allowed to validate the correction on data for circular cross section pipe with a constant property flow. However, it can be expected that the approach will improve the prediction for rectangular cross section channels and variable property flows as well. More in general, the prediction by simulations addressing the actual complex geometries of LRE cooling channels, typically featuring high aspect ratio, curved rectangular channels, asymmetrical heating conditions can
be improved by the present model. Designers can largely benefit from this approach which can be simply implemented in the Spalart-Allmaras model, which is a basic and widespread closure of Reynolds Averaged Navier-Stokes equations. It will allow to reduce the risk of overestimation of cooling capabilities and at the same time take into account phenomena like thermal stratification that cannot easily be considered in one-dimensional approaches.
